WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.491
The grocer's van has been.

2
00:00:04.491 --> 00:00:10.170
With the shopping comes a delivery note, one line per item, like flour, 2000, grams.

3
00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:15.105
Each line is an item to put away, and a line to write in the pantry book.

4
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:19.236
The note is read by the old reader from Flow basics.

5
00:00:19.236 --> 00:00:21.978
Its readLine holds its thread while it waits.

6
00:00:21.978 --> 00:00:26.732
That's slow, waiting work, and it belongs in the back room, on another thread.

7
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:29.618
But the pantry book stays at the kitchen table.

8
00:00:29.618 --> 00:00:34.887
It isn't thread-safe: two threads writing in it at the same moment can mix up their lines.

9
00:00:34.887 --> 00:00:37.404
So all the writing must happen on one side.

10
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.631
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

11
00:00:40.631 --> 00:00:45.704
For any other slow call, we'd use withContext to run it on the IO dispatcher.

12
00:00:45.704 --> 00:00:49.460
So why not wrap the read and the emit in withContext(io)?

13
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:57.600
Because the inspector from lesson 2 refuses it: Flow invariant is violated.

14
00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.832
And it's right to.

15
00:00:58.832 --> 00:01:01.433
The collector's code runs inside emit.

16
00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:07.320
So an emit on an IO thread drags the collector, pantry book and all, onto that thread.

17
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:15.432
A hand-written flow with no inspector shows it: its collector ran on DefaultDispatcher-worker-1, a thread it never asked for.

18
00:01:15.432 --> 00:01:19.192
That's carrying the pantry book into the back room by hand.

19
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.193
The fix is one line: flowOn(io).

20
00:01:22.193 --> 00:01:25.980
Putting an item away takes the collector 30 milliseconds.

21
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.701
Without flowOn, read and stored take turns, all on main.

22
00:01:29.701 --> 00:01:32.957
With it, in what order do the six lines come out?

23
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.818
All three reads come first, on io-thread.

24
00:01:38.818 --> 00:01:40.797
Then the three stores, on main.

25
00:01:40.797 --> 00:01:45.905
The reader ran ahead: its emit calls returned without waiting for the collector.

26
00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:49.828
In lesson 1, emit couldn't return before the collector did.

27
00:01:49.828 --> 00:01:53.781
So something now sits between the two sides, and holds values.

28
00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:58.740
Kitchens solved this long ago, with a wall and a hatch in it.

29
00:01:58.740 --> 00:01:59.941
Plates go through.

30
00:01:59.941 --> 00:02:00.809
People don't.

31
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:01.885
Let's map it.

32
00:02:01.885 --> 00:02:03.723
flowOn(io) builds the wall.

33
00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:10.600
Behind it, a second cook, which is a second coroutine, runs everything above flowOn on the IO thread.

34
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.323
In the hatch sits a shelf with 64 spots.

35
00:02:13.323 --> 00:02:18.633
That's a channel: a queue between coroutines, where send waits when it's full.

36
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.504
In front of the wall, a loop takes each item off the shelf, and calls emit in the collector's own coroutine.

37
00:02:25.504 --> 00:02:28.421
So the inspector has nothing to complain about.

38
00:02:28.421 --> 00:02:31.215
The collector and the pantry book never move.

39
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:33.982
How far ahead can the reader get?

40
00:02:33.982 --> 00:02:37.583
Say the collector holds the first item and doesn't let go.

41
00:02:37.583 --> 00:02:39.569
How many emits return meanwhile?

42
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.409
In a plain flow, zero.

43
00:02:44.409 --> 00:02:49.603
With flowOn(io), 65: 64 on the shelf, and one in the collector's hands.

44
00:02:49.603 --> 00:02:53.920
Then the sixty-sixth send waits, because the shelf is full.

45
00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.992
Shrink the shelf to 10 spots, and it's 11.

46
00:02:57.533 --> 00:03:02.353
So flowOn doesn't remove backpressure, the slow side slowing the fast one.

47
00:03:02.353 --> 00:03:07.042
It moves it: from waiting for the collector, to waiting for a free spot.

48
00:03:07.042 --> 00:03:11.015
A slow collector still slows the reader, just 64 items later.

49
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.795
Here's the rule to keep.

50
00:03:12.795 --> 00:03:15.785
flowOn moves the code above it behind a wall.

51
00:03:15.785 --> 00:03:17.513
The collector never moves.

52
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.603
So read a chain from the bottom up.

53
00:03:20.603 --> 00:03:26.078
Put the pantry writes above flowOn, and the pantry book quietly moves to the back room.

54
00:03:26.078 --> 00:03:27.399
Nobody told the book.

55
00:03:27.399 --> 00:03:30.672
Put flowOn right below the last line that must move.

56
00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:33.362
The picture breaks in one place.

57
00:03:33.362 --> 00:03:35.436
A real hatch just sits in the wall.

58
00:03:35.436 --> 00:03:42.900
This one makes the cook behind it wait when the shelf is full, and when the collector stops, the whole back room is cancelled.

59
00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:49.765
One more kitchen: two cooks, at the grill and the oven, and one diner who wants plates from both.

60
00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:54.480
The obvious code launches two children inside flow { }, and both call emit.

61
00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.692
The inspector refuses: emission from another coroutine is detected.

62
00:03:58.692 --> 00:04:03.469
Two children could emit at the same moment, and run the diner twice at once.

63
00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:08.177
The message names the cure: channelFlow, a flow with a hatch built in.

64
00:04:08.177 --> 00:04:14.620
Each cook calls send, which is safe from any child, and the flow ends only when both cooks are done.

65
00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.231
Several cooks, one hatch.

66
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.611
Here's the delivery import in Kotlin: read and parse above flowOn, store below it.

67
00:04:22.611 --> 00:04:30.069
And the Java twin: a reader thread puts items into a queue with 64 slots, and the main thread takes them out.

68
00:04:30.867 --> 00:04:33.275
So, why not withContext around emit?

69
00:04:33.275 --> 00:04:37.756
Because the collector runs inside emit, and would be dragged along.

70
00:04:37.756 --> 00:04:45.180
flowOn builds a wall instead: a second cook behind it, a hatch with 64 spots, and a collector that never moves.

71
00:04:45.333 --> 00:04:46.260
Quick check.

72
00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:49.272
In this chain, where do a, b and c run?

73
00:04:52.533 --> 00:04:55.667
a runs on io, because it sits above flowOn.

74
00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:58.509
b and c run in the collector's context.

75
00:04:58.509 --> 00:05:00.404
flowOn only ever looks up.
